        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Coccinia abyssinica § Use",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coccinia_abyssinica#Use",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1188105598,
        "heading": "Use",
        "text": "Coccinia abyssinica tubers are an important staple crop in the Ethiopian highlands. The tubers are rich in starch and are cooked. Also the young shoots and the leaves are cooked and eaten around Dembi Dolo, Oromia State. There are also beliefs about medical use, but none is scientifically verified so far. The relatively high content of calcium might be the reason for the local belief that eating C. abyssinica helps against fractured bones. The edibility of the fruits is disputed, eventually, there are different races.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
